WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.989
Model selection is a resource-matching exercise.

00:00:02.989 --> 00:00:13.151
Parameter count provides a rough indication of model size, but the loaded footprint also depends on numerical precision, quantization, context length, and runtime overhead.

00:00:13.151 --> 00:00:21.720
A model that nearly fills VRAM before receiving a prompt may run out of memory as its context cache grows.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.498
Leave operating margin instead of treating published file size as the complete requirement.

00:00:27.498 --> 00:00:34.473
Quantization reduces the precision used to store model weights, lowering memory demand and often increasing speed.

00:00:34.473 --> 00:00:40.650
The tradeoff can include some loss of output quality, especially at very aggressive settings.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:51.211
A well-chosen quantized model that remains on the GPU often produces a better interactive experience than a larger model split heavily across slower system memory.

00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:57.786
Test more than one quantization when quality matters, using prompts that reflect the real task.

00:00:57.786 --> 00:01:01.174
Choose the model family for the workload.

00:01:01.174 --> 00:01:05.757
General chat models are useful for drafting, explanation, and summarization.

00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:08.347
Coding models prioritize software tasks.

00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:13.728
Vision-language models accept images but require additional memory and compatible runtime support.

00:01:13.728 --> 00:01:24.289
Embedding models convert text into vectors for search and retrieval; they are usually smaller and should be selected for language, retrieval quality, and application compatibility.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:28.473
Do not assume one model must perform every role.

00:01:28.473 --> 00:01:37.041
Context length controls how much text the model can consider, but larger settings consume memory and can slow prompt processing.

00:01:37.041 --> 00:01:40.827
Set context to what the workflow actually needs.

00:01:40.827 --> 00:01:49.794
Begin with a smaller, well-supported model that runs reliably, record speed and memory use, and evaluate answer quality against representative tasks.

00:01:49.794 --> 00:01:54.776
Scale upward only when the smaller model fails a meaningful requirement.

00:01:54.776 --> 00:02:03.344
A stable model that fits the workstation and consistently completes the job is the right model, regardless of leaderboard fashion.
